<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Medri Bahri</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1890 CE (approx.) · capital Debarwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The highland kingdom of the 'sea land' in today's Eritrea, ruled by the Bahr Negash as a semi-independent vassal of the Ethiopian emperors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The region north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mareb-river">Mareb</a> river was known as Medri Bahri, the land of the sea, and from the 14th century its ruler bore the title Bahr Negash, king of the sea, under the Solomonic emperors of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. The office gained strength in the 15th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>, and its seat became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debarwa">Debarwa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Bahr Negash governed the Tigrinya-speaking highlands, collecting tribute and guarding the approaches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast. Society was organised around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian villages, monasteries and landholding kin groups with customary codes. In the 1560s and 1570s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeshaq-bahr-negash">Bahr Negash Yeshaq</a> allied with the Ottoman <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a> against the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarsa-dengel">Sarsa Dengel</a>, showing the province's frequent independence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The office continued under various local dynasties, contested by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a> lords, Egyptian forces and the Ethiopian state. After the Italians occupied the highlands, the territory became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a> in 1890, and the old title lapsed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/medri-bahri.jpg" alt="Medri Bahri" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Taddesse Tamrat · PDM-owner · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Christian_Empire_on_the_Eve_of_the_Wars_of_Ahmad_Gragn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1890 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Debarwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yeshaq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tigrinya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bahr Negash, Midri Bahri, Mareb Melash</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1620&amp;focus=medri-bahri" aria-label="Medri Bahri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5741 1616 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Medri Bahri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6061 1872L6029 1964M6061 1872L6076 1854M6061 1872L6063 1863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0M6076 1854h0M6063 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6061 1872h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6061 1872h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Debarwa. <a href="/atlas?year=1620&amp;focus=medri-bahri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
